  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington delivered a strong day yesterday\u2014$3,740 total revenue, up 68% versus last Tuesday, driven by genuine capacity gains and a retail spike. With 5 groomers on the floor producing $525 per groomer (above the 30-day baseline of $497), the team ran hot. Core grooming revenue climbed 40% week-over-week; retail jumped 268%, suggesting front-of-house is executing on add-on and impulse sells. Month-to-date Port is at $81,740, which is only 2.4% below DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014calendar mix, not performance, accounts for the miss. Year-to-date you're up 47% versus 2025. One concern: add-on attach fell from 73% to 44% yesterday, signaling possible upsell discipline slipping; watch this metric closely over the next few days.\n\nHicksville is the stronger story operationally. Yesterday's $1,297 revenue (up 147% YoY) on just 2 groomer-days at $478 per groomer significantly outpaced the 30-day baseline of $413\u2014these two groomers are firing. Month-to-date Hicksville is actually 5.1% *above* DOW-adjusted expectation, showing real performance gain. However, the visit-cadence drift is moving the wrong direction: the 12+ week gap bucket grew from 10.2% to 12.6%, a leading indicator that customers are stretching appointments and stealth churn may be starting. This is the one thing to pay attention to\u2014retention is softening while capacity is constrained.\n\nBoth locations face an inventory crunch: X-Small Treat Table is critically depleted at both stores (0 left in Port, -139 in Hicksville), and premium retail items are negative across the board. Reorder immediately. Port Washington has zero bather hours yesterday despite scheduling 4.3 on average\u2014clarify whether bather coverage is being underutilized or if scheduling is aspirational.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington staffed 6 groomers today with 7.0h bathers; Hicksville 3 groomers with 0h bathers; next 7 days 49 groomer-days combined and 43h bather hours.**"
